;; kanata config — port of the old Karabiner-Elements rules plus home row mods.
;;
;; Design notes:
;;   * Home row mods are the reason for switching; everything else is carried
;;     over from Karabiner as faithfully as kanata allows.
;;   * kanata has NO frontmost-application awareness. The Karabiner rules that
;;     used `frontmost_application_unless` (Ctrl-W, Ctrl-U) are deliberately
;;     NOT ported. In the terminal, Ctrl-W and Ctrl-U already do delete-word /
;;     delete-line natively in both zsh and nvim insert mode, which is exactly
;;     what those exclusions were protecting. Outside the terminal, use
;;     Ctrl-Opt-H (delete word) and Ctrl-Cmd-H (delete line).
;;   * `unmod` below drops Ctrl but leaves Opt/Cmd held, reproducing Karabiner's
;;     `optional: ["any"]` modifier passthrough.
;;   * Chordal hold means SAME-HAND combos never fire the modifier, so reach
;;     for the Ctrl on the OPPOSITE hand from the letter:
;;         Ctrl-W  -> `l`+w   (w is left-hand, so use right Ctrl)
;;         Ctrl-H  -> `s`+h   (h is right-hand, so use left Ctrl)
;;         Ctrl-U  -> `s`+u     Ctrl-M -> `s`+m     Ctrl-N/P -> `s`+n / `s`+p
;;     Physical bottom-left Ctrl is the escape hatch that always works
;;     regardless of which hand the letter is on. (Caps Lock used to be that
;;     hatch; it is a plain Esc now -- the Voyager has KC_ESCAPE in exactly
;;     that position, and home row Ctrl made a second hatch redundant.)
;;   * Layer keys are THUMB keys, mirroring the Voyager's LT() thumbs 1:1:
;;         spc   tap Space  / hold symbols   (Voyager: LT(1, KC_SPACE))
;;         rmet  tap Return / hold nav       (Voyager: LT(2, KC_ENTER))
;;         ralt  hold launch                 (real F1-F12, layout toggle)
;;     They used to sit on the right index (`m` and `n`), which is exactly why
;;     the symbol layer's payload had to be confined to the left hand. On a
;;     thumb BOTH hands are free, so the numpad half of the Voyager's Sym+Num
;;     layer fits and the digits stop being a two-row-of-five invention.
;;     Physical Right Option is consumed outright by `launch`; Opt still comes
;;     from Left Option and from the home row mods on either hand.
;;   * The app launcher needs NO kanata support at all. skhd binds the RAW
;;     chord (`cmd + ctrl + alt + shift - 1`), the Voyager sends Hyper from a
;;     thumb, and here Hyper is four home row mods held at once: a+s+d+f in
;;     QWERTY, a+r+s+t in Colemak. Then a digit on the number row. A `launch`
;;     row of @app1..@app7 emitting M-A-C-S-<n> used to exist; it was a second
;;     path to a keystroke the home row already produces, so it is gone.
;;   * The function row is remapped BACK to its macOS media functions. This is
;;     not a preference, it's a repair -- see the deflayer comment below.
;;   * In QWERTY, Left Shift is a plain Left Shift. There was an experiment
;;     making it a literal `b` (bottom row reading b z x c v b, left-hand Shift
;;     coming from holding `a`); it is commented out in the base deflayer and in
;;     `left-hand-keys`, and both halves have to be re-enabled together.
;;   * There are TWO alpha layouts: `base` (QWERTY) and `colemak` (Colemak-DH
;;     with the left-hand angle mod). Hold Right Option and tap `\` to flip.
;;     kanata boots into QWERTY, because `base` is the first deflayer.
;;     Everything positional is identical in both -- home row mod placement, the
;;     thumb layer keys, the function row, Caps-as-Esc, and the entire right
;;     half of the symbol layer -- so only the letters and the two Ctrl rewrites
;;     that follow them actually move.

(defcfg
  process-unmapped-keys yes   ;; keys absent from defsrc pass through untouched
  concurrent-tap-hold yes     ;; required to chord two home row mods at once

  ;; Built-in keyboard ONLY. The Voyager has its own firmware mods and layers;
  ;; letting kanata also apply home row mods to it would double-process every
  ;; keystroke. Names come from `kanata --list`.
  macos-dev-names-include (
    "Apple Internal Keyboard / Trackpad"
  )
)

(defsrc
  esc  f1   f2   f3   f4   f5   f6   f7   f8   f9   f10  f11  f12
  grv  1    2    3    4    5    6    7    8    9    0    -    =    bspc
  tab  q    w    e    r    t    y    u    i    o    p    [    ]    \
  caps a    s    d    f    g    h    j    k    l    ;    '    ret
  lsft z    x    c    v    b    n    m    ,    .    /    rsft
  lctl lalt lmet spc  rmet ralt
)

(defvar
  ;; tap-time: how long after release a re-press still counts as a tap
  ;; hold-time: how long to hold before the modifier engages
  tap-time  200
  hold-time 200

  ;; Chordal hold ("opposite hand rule"): pressing one of these while the
  ;; same-hand mod is held settles the mod as a TAP, so same-hand rolls
  ;; (e.g. "as", "de", "jk") type letters instead of firing modifiers.
  ;; Mirrors QMK's chordal_hold_layout, which Oryx guesses from geometry.
  ;;
  ;; The home row mod keys themselves (a s d f / j k l ;) are deliberately
  ;; ABSENT from their own hand's list, so that same-hand mods can be CHAINED:
  ;; s+f+h is Ctrl-Cmd-H (delete to line start). With `f` in the left list,
  ;; pressing it early-tapped `s` into a literal "s" and left Cmd-H = Hide
  ;; Window. QMK does the same thing -- "as an exception to the opposite hands
  ;; rule, Chordal Hold supports combining multiple same-side modifiers within
  ;; the tapping term" -- so this is closer to the Voyager, not further.
  ;; Cost: a genuinely slow same-hand roll (holding `a` past 200ms while
  ;; hitting `s`) can fire Shift. The Voyager has that same exposure.
  ;; The commented-out `lsft` below belongs to the disabled "Left Shift is a
  ;; literal `b`" experiment (see the deflayer). It is listed only while that is
  ;; on: with Left Shift emitting `b` it stops being a modifier and becomes an
  ;; ordinary left-hand letter key, so the opposite-hand rule has to cover it
  ;; like any other. (These lists name PHYSICAL defsrc keys, not what they emit,
  ;; which is why it reads `lsft` rather than a second `b`.) Without it, holding
  ;; `a` past 200ms and then hitting it fired Shift and produced `B`, where the
  ;; real `b` one row over would have early-tapped `a` and typed "ab".
  ;; While Left Shift is a real Shift it stays commented out: a modifier belongs
  ;; in neither list, same as `a s d f` are absent from their own hand's.
  left-hand-keys (
    ;; lsft
    q w e r t
    g
    z x c v b
  )
  right-hand-keys (
    y u i o p
    h
    n m , . /
  )

  ;; Colemak's angle mod turns Left Shift into a literal `z`, so there it stops
  ;; being a modifier and becomes an ordinary left-hand letter key -- the
  ;; opposite-hand rule has to cover it like any other. This is the same reasoning
  ;; the disabled `b` experiment above needed, now permanently live but scoped to
  ;; one layout instead of both. QWERTY's mods keep `$left-hand-keys`, where Left
  ;; Shift is a real modifier and so belongs in neither list.
  ;; @cmz itself also uses `$left-hand-keys`, NOT this one: a key is always absent
  ;; from its own hand's list, so Shift can still chain with the other left-side
  ;; mods (lsft + hold `t` = Shift-Cmd).
  left-hand-keys-cmk (
    lsft
    q w e r t
    g
    z x c v b
  )
)

(defalias
  ;; ---- Ctrl-<key> rewrites (BUILT-IN KEYBOARD ONLY; no app conditions) ----
  ;; @ctlh sits on physical h in QWERTY and on physical m in Colemak; @ctlm the
  ;; other way round. Both are plain forks -- see the note above @ctlm.
  ;; "global" here means every app, NOT every keyboard. macos-dev-names-include
  ;; scopes this whole config to the laptop, so on the Voyager these forks do
  ;; not exist -- .config/skhd/skhdrc rewrites the same chords at the event-tap
  ;; level to cover it. Change one, change the other. That copy is the fragile
  ;; half (it synthesizes events and the held Ctrl leaks into them); see the
  ;; warning in README.md before adding a chord to either file.
  ;; (fork <normal> <alternate> <trigger-keys>)
  ;; Ctrl held -> alternate. `unmod` releases only Ctrl, so Opt and Cmd
  ;; still reach the output:
  ;;   Ctrl-H      -> Delete
  ;;   Ctrl-Opt-H  -> Opt-Delete   (delete word)
  ;;   Ctrl-Cmd-H  -> Cmd-Delete   (delete to line start)
  ;; EXCEPT under Hyper. Hyper is all four mods, so it contains Ctrl, and a
  ;; plain fork would turn Hyper-H into Cmd-Opt-Shift-Delete -- Hyper-H could
  ;; never leave the laptop. Hyper-H is the macOS dictation shortcut (set in
  ;; System Settings > Keyboard > Dictation, so no skhd line is involved and
  ;; the Voyager gets it for free), hence the `switch`: Hyper -> plain h,
  ;; any other Ctrl chord -> the fork's old alternate, else -> h.
  ctlh (switch
    ((and (or lctl rctl) (or lmet rmet) (or lalt ralt) (or lsft rsft))) h break
    ((or lctl rctl)) (unmod (lctl rctl) bspc) break
    () h break)
  ctlb (fork [ (unmod (lctl rctl) esc)  (lctl rctl))
  ;; Ctrl-M -> Return is @ctlm, defined further down with the Colemak block
  ;; because that is where the h/m swap is explained. It is the same plain shape
  ;; as these two; it used to have to wrap a tap-hold, and no longer does.
  ;; Ctrl-; -> ~ lives in defoverrides at the bottom: `unmod` only accepts
  ;; plain key names, not shifted forms like S-grv.

  ;; ---- Ctrl-N / Ctrl-P -> Down / Up  (PENDING DECISION, not wired up) ----
  ;; Was Spotlight list navigation in Karabiner, excluded in terminals.
  ;; Going global costs exactly one thing: nvim insert-mode keyword
  ;; completion (verified — <C-n> completes, <Down> does not). Recover it
  ;; with a line in init.lua:
  ;;     vim.keymap.set("i", "<C-Space>", "<C-n>")
  ;; Skip all of this if you switch to Raycast, which navigates with
  ;; Ctrl-N/P natively. To enable: uncomment these two and drop @ctln / @ctlp in
  ;; place of `n` and `p` in the base layer -- two straight substitutions. This
  ;; used to be fiddlier: `n` WAS the nav layer key, so @ctln had to be threaded
  ;; in as its tap action. Nav is on a thumb now and `n` is a plain key again.
  ;; ctln (fork n (unmod (lctl rctl) down) (lctl rctl))
  ;; ctlp (fork p (unmod (lctl rctl) up)   (lctl rctl))

  ;; ---- Home row mods, index finger inward: Cmd Opt Ctrl Shift ----
  ;; `tap-hold-tap-keys`, NOT `tap-hold-release-keys`, so that both keyboards
  ;; feel the same. The Voyager runs QMK Chordal Hold with Permissive Hold and
  ;; Hold-On-Other-Key-Press both OFF (Oryx defaults), and QMK's docs are
  ;; explicit that with those off, "if the keys are on opposite hands, Chordal
  ;; Hold introduces no new behavior" -- a cross-hand roll finished inside the
  ;; tapping term is a TAP, and the mod only fires past the full timeout.
  ;; `tap-hold-release-keys` is Permissive Hold ("activate hold early if
  ;; another key is pressed and released"), so it fired modifiers on fast
  ;; cross-hand rolls that the Voyager types straight through.
  ;; `tap-hold-tap-keys` has the identical signature but waits out the full
  ;; $hold-time, which is the QMK-default behaviour.
  ;;
  ;; Cost of parity: deliberate mods are slower. Ctrl-W is hold `l` for the
  ;; full 200ms, THEN w -- you can't roll into it. Same as on the Voyager.
  ;;
  ;; Do NOT add `tap-hold-require-prior-idle` (ZMK's require-prior-idle-ms /
  ;; QMK's Flow Tap). It would cut misfires further, but FLOW_TAP_TERM is off
  ;; on the Voyager, so it would make the two keyboards diverge again. If it's
  ;; ever wanted, turn it on in Oryx first.
  ;; $tap-time here is the tap-repress timeout = QMK's QUICK_TAP_TERM (200),
  ;; and $hold-time = TAPPING_TERM (200). Both already match Oryx.
  hma (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time a     lsft $left-hand-keys)
  hms (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time s     lctl $left-hand-keys)
  hmd (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time d     lalt $left-hand-keys)
  hmf (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time f     lmet $left-hand-keys)
  hmj (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time j     rmet $right-hand-keys)
  hmk (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time k     ralt $right-hand-keys)
  hml (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time l     rctl $right-hand-keys)
  hmsc (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time ;     rsft $right-hand-keys)

  ;; ---- The same eight mods, with Colemak-DH letters under them ----
  ;; Physical a s d f / j k l ; carry Shift Ctrl Opt Cmd inward exactly as above;
  ;; only the tap letters change (a r s t / n e i o). Timings, chordal behaviour
  ;; and every argument in the block above are unchanged -- if you retune one set
  ;; of eight, retune the other, or the two layouts stop feeling alike.
  cma (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time a lsft $left-hand-keys-cmk)
  cmr (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time r lctl $left-hand-keys-cmk)
  cms (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time s lalt $left-hand-keys-cmk)
  cmt (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time t lmet $left-hand-keys-cmk)
  cmn (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time n rmet $right-hand-keys)
  cme (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time e ralt $right-hand-keys)
  cmi (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time i rctl $right-hand-keys)
  cmo (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time o rsft $right-hand-keys)

  ;; Angle mod: the left bottom row slides one key left onto Left Shift, so that
  ;; key becomes `z`. Tap-hold rather than a plain `z` because the alternative is
  ;; strictly worse -- with a plain `z` the ONLY left Shift left is hold-`a`,
  ;; which is already a 200ms tap-hold, so nothing is gained and the pinky Shift
  ;; is lost. Cost: `z` loses key repeat, and dawdling on it past $hold-time
  ;; fires Shift. Shifting a LEFT-hand letter early-taps to `z` by design -- use
  ;; the opposite hand for capitals, same rule as everywhere else here.
  ;; This does NOT interfere with `<hold spc>z` -> Tab: a held layer's entry
  ;; replaces the base action outright, so the tap-hold underneath is never
  ;; consulted. See the `symbols-cmk` layer for where Tab actually sits.
  cmz (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time z lsft $left-hand-keys)

  ;; Colemak swaps the letters h and m, and the Ctrl rewrites follow the LETTER,
  ;; not the physical key, so the two forks simply trade physical keys:
  ;;   QWERTY   physical h -> @ctlh (Delete)   physical m -> @ctlm (Return)
  ;;   Colemak  physical h -> @ctlm (Return)   physical m -> @ctlh (Delete)
  ;; Both are BARE forks. They did not used to be: whichever physical key was
  ;; also the symbols layer key had to wrap its tap-hold in the fork and resolve
  ;; on press, which cost that key its repeat and made a held Ctrl repeat
  ;; Return/Delete instead of opening the layer. Moving the layer onto the thumb
  ;; deleted @symm and @symh outright and left these two, which is the single
  ;; largest simplification the thumb move bought.
  ;; No Colemak twin is needed for the other two rewrites: `[` doesn't move, and
  ;; Ctrl-; -> ~ follows `;` to physical p by itself, because defoverrides match
  ;; on OUTPUT keys rather than physical ones.
  ctlm (fork m (unmod (lctl rctl) ret) (lctl rctl))

  ;; ---- Layout toggle: hold Right Option, tap `\` ----
  ;; `layer-switch` on its own is absolute, not a toggle, so `switch` reads the
  ;; current base layer and picks the other one. That is what buys a single key
  ;; instead of a separate force-QWERTY and force-Colemak pair.
  ;; Cost of one key: nothing signals which layout is live -- the tell is that
  ;; you start typing garbage, and the fix is to press it again. kanata boots
  ;; into `base` because it is the first deflayer, so a restart (or the Homebrew
  ;; upgrade that kills the service; see README) is always a known state.
  tog (switch
        ((base-layer base)) (layer-switch colemak) break
        ()                  (layer-switch base)    break)

  ;; ---- Thumb layer keys ----
  ;; The Voyager holds both of its layers with a thumb -- LT(1, KC_SPACE) left,
  ;; LT(2, KC_ENTER) right -- and its chordal_hold_layout marks all four thumb
  ;; keys '*', which in QMK means "hold is always allowed here, the opposite-
  ;; hand rule does not apply". Plain `tap-hold` with NO key list is the exact
  ;; kanata equivalent, which is why these deliberately do not take
  ;; $left-hand-keys / $right-hand-keys the way every mod above does.
  ;;
  ;; @spc and @spck differ only in which symbols layer they open. The base layer
  ;; picks, exactly as it used to pick between @symm and @symh.
  ;;
  ;; KNOWN RISK, taken deliberately: Space is the most-pressed key on the board,
  ;; and any press held past $hold-time now opens a layer instead of doing
  ;; nothing. The Voyager has run precisely this arrangement without trouble,
  ;; which is the whole argument for trying it. If it does misfire in practice,
  ;; the cheap knob is a longer hold time on THIS key alone -- write a literal
  ;; 250 or 300 in place of $hold-time here and leave every other timing at 200,
  ;; since this is the only key whose hold is fighting ordinary prose. The
  ;; expensive fix is moving symbols back off the thumb, which costs the numpad.
  ;; Space also loses key repeat; it already had, under the old launcher.
  spc  (tap-hold $tap-time $hold-time spc (layer-while-held symbols))
  spck (tap-hold $tap-time $hold-time spc (layer-while-held symbols-cmk))

  ;; Right Cmd: tap Return, hold nav -- the Voyager's LT(2, KC_ENTER). The same
  ;; key carries `0` on the symbol layer, which is NOT a conflict: a held
  ;; layer's entry replaces the base action outright, so this tap-hold is never
  ;; consulted while Space is down. Same mechanism as `<hold spc>z` -> Tab.
  ;; Physical Right Cmd is consumed. Cmd survives on physical Left Cmd, on
  ;; @hmf/@cmt (left hand) and on @hmj/@cmn (right hand).
  nav  (tap-hold $tap-time $hold-time ret (layer-while-held nav))

  ;; Right Option: the launch layer and nothing else. No tap action, so it is a
  ;; plain layer key with no timing behaviour at all -- press and it is open,
  ;; which is what the F-keys and the layout toggle want.
  ;; Physical Right Option is consumed. Opt survives on physical Left Option and
  ;; on @hmd/@cms (left hand) and @hmk/@cme (right hand).
  lch  (layer-while-held launch)

  ;; ---- Numpad home row mods ----
  ;; The Voyager keeps mod-taps live on its numpad -- MT(MOD_RGUI, KC_4),
  ;; MT(MOD_RALT, KC_5), MT(MOD_RCTL, KC_6), and Shift under `*` -- so
  ;; Cmd/Opt/Ctrl/Shift stay chordable without leaving the layer. Mirrored here
  ;; on the same physical keys that carry them in the alphas (j k l ;), in the
  ;; same index-inward Cmd-Opt-Ctrl-Shift order, with the same $right-hand-keys
  ;; chordal list -- so a roll like "456" settles as taps and typing digits is
  ;; no slower than typing letters.
  ;; Cost: holding 4/5/6/* no longer key-repeats. Physical j is deliberately
  ;; skipped in the Voyager's own numpad too (`+` there is a plain key).
  np4 (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time 4   rmet $right-hand-keys)
  np5 (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time 5   ralt $right-hand-keys)
  np6 (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time 6   rctl $right-hand-keys)
  npx (tap-hold-tap-keys $tap-time $hold-time S-8 rsft $right-hand-keys)
)

;; Ctrl-; -> ~  (exact input-combo -> output-combo rewrite)
(defoverrides
  (lctl ;) (lsft grv)
  (rctl ;) (lsft grv)
)

;; Function row: restore the macOS media keys.
;;
;; Karabiner's driver seizes the keyboard BEFORE macOS translates the F-row into
;; brightness/volume/etc. That translation is done by macOS's Apple-keyboard HID
;; driver and only applies to real Apple keyboards, so kanata receives a plain
;; F1..F12 and re-emits it on the Karabiner *virtual* keyboard, which macOS does
;; not treat as an Apple keyboard. Result: every media key silently becomes a
;; dead F-key. Karabiner-Elements did this translation itself, which is why the
;; keys worked before the switch. kanata won't, so do it here.
;; (upstream: jtroo/kanata#1141, #975 -- macOS is unmaintained there)
;;
;; F3/F4 have no HID usage kanata can emit, so they send the equivalent macOS
;; shortcut instead; they break if those shortcuts are ever rebound.
;; F5 (dictation) and F6 (Focus) have neither a usage nor a default shortcut,
;; so they stay dead -- same as they are right now.
;; Real F1..F12 live on the `launch` layer: hold Right Option and press the key.
;;
;; Top-left physical Esc is Caps Lock: the caps-position key already types Esc
;; (matching the Voyager), so this key was a duplicate -- and losing the real
;; Caps Lock turned out to sting once Shift moved to the home row.
(deflayer base
  caps brdn brup C-up M-spc f5  f6   prev pp   next mute vold volu
  grv  1    2    3    4    5    6    7    8    9    0    -    =    bspc
  tab  q    w    e    r    t    y    u    i    o    p    @ctlb ]   \
  esc  @hma @hms @hmd @hmf g    @ctlh @hmj @hmk @hml @hmsc '   ret
  ;; Left Shift as a literal `b` -- disabled, kept for reference. To re-enable,
  ;; swap the two rows below and re-enable the `lsft` entry in `left-hand-keys`.
  ;; b    z    x    c    v    b    n     @ctlm ,  .    /    rsft
  lsft z    x    c    v    b    n     @ctlm ,  .    /    rsft
  lctl lalt lmet @spc @nav @lch
)

;; Colemak-DH with the left-hand angle mod. Space+`\` toggles here and back.
;;
;;   tab   q w f p b   j l u y ;   [ ] \
;;   ctrl  a r s t g   m n e i o   ' ret
;;   z     x c d v _   k h , . /   shift
;;
;; The angle mod slides the left bottom row one key LEFT, onto Left Shift, so it
;; is reached at an angle instead of a claw. Physical `lsft z x c v` type
;; `z x c d v`, and physical `b` falls off the end with nothing to fill it --
;; Colemak-DH puts `b` on physical `t`, and the index-inward stretch has no other
;; claimant. Left dead (XX) on purpose: during the transition a mis-reach there
;; should be silent rather than quietly typing a letter. Swap the XX to `v` if
;; that turns out to be more annoying than a doubled letter.
;;
;; The right hand is NOT angled -- the angle mod is a left-hand fix -- so the
;; entire right half is positional and byte-identical to QWERTY's, including the
;; numpad on the symbol layer. The nav layer is shared verbatim as well.
;; `symbols-cmk` exists only because the symbol layer's LEFT half sits on the
;; bottom row the angle mod shifts; its right half is a copy.
;;
;; Untouched from `base`: the function row, the number row, Caps-as-Esc, all
;; three thumb layer keys, and Ctrl-[ -> Esc. The Ctrl rewrites follow their
;; LETTERS, so the two forks simply swap physical keys -- @ctlm on physical h,
;; @ctlh on physical m.
(deflayer colemak
  caps brdn brup C-up M-spc f5  f6   prev pp   next mute vold volu
  grv  1    2    3    4    5    6    7    8    9    0    -    =    bspc
  tab  q    w    f    p    b    j    l    u    y    ;    @ctlb ]   \
  esc  @cma @cmr @cms @cmt g    @ctlm @cmn @cme @cmi @cmo '   ret
  @cmz x    c    d    v    XX   k     @ctlh ,  .    /    rsft
  lctl lalt lmet @spck @nav @lch
)

;; Space-hold: the Voyager's Sym+Num layer, BOTH halves. Symbols on the left
;; hand, a real numpad on the right, laid out exactly as QMK layer 1 has it:
;;
;;     ! @ # $ %        -  7 8 9 =      <- q w e r t  /  y u i o p
;;     ^ & * ( )        +  4 5 6 *      <- a s d f g  /  h j k l ;
;;     tab [ ] `        .  1 2 3 /      <- z x c v    /  n m , . /
;;                            0         <- rmet, the right thumb
;;
;; The right half is why the layer key had to leave the right index and move to
;; the thumb -- it is the payload that hand used to be holding. It is also
;; PURELY POSITIONAL: physical u i o / j k l / m , . spell 7 8 9 / 4 5 6 /
;; 1 2 3 whichever alpha layout is live, so this half is duplicated verbatim in
;; `symbols-cmk` and the two must be edited together.
;;
;; Digits are literal digits now. They used to exist only as Shift + a
;; left-hand glyph, through a rank of ten @sy1..@sy0 forks that peeled Shift
;; back off with `unmod`; all ten are deleted, and so is the caveat that Shift
;; itself then had to come from `;` or a physical Shift key. `S-<n>` survives on
;; the LEFT hand only because it keeps the mapping to the number row legible at
;; a glance -- there is no Shift trick left to read into it.
;;
;; The left hand's home row mods are shadowed, deliberately: that half is for
;; typing, not chording. The right hand's are kept (@np4..@npx), matching the
;; Voyager. One consequence for the launcher, since Hyper is a+s+d+f and those
;; four are shadowed here: press the home row keys FIRST and Space after if you
;; ever want Hyper plus a numpad digit. Hyper plus the NUMBER ROW involves no
;; layer at all and is the ordinary path.
(deflayer symbols
  _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _
  _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _
  _    S-1  S-2  S-3  S-4  S-5  -    7    8    9    =    _    _    _
  _    S-6  S-7  S-8  S-9  S-0  S-=  @np4 @np5 @np6 @npx _   _
  _    tab  [    ]    grv  _    .    1    2    3    /    _
  _    _    _    _    0    _
)

;; The same layer with its LEFT bottom row angled, so the symbols stay under the
;; same fingers once the alphas shift. `base` points @spc at `symbols`, `colemak`
;; points @spck here; the base layer picks, so there is nothing extra to switch.
;; Exactly one row differs from `symbols` -- the bottom one, and only its left
;; half, because that is all the angle mod touches:
;;
;;     ! @ # $ %        -  7 8 9 =      <- q w e r t  /  y u i o p
;;     ^ & * ( )        +  4 5 6 *      <- a r s t g  /  h j k l ;
;;   tab [ ] `          .  1 2 3 /      <- lsft z x c /  n m , . /
;;                            0         <- rmet, the right thumb
;;
;; The right half is a verbatim copy of `symbols` and has to stay that way; see
;; the note there. Nothing on this layer needs Shift any more, so the old
;; warning about Left Shift being unreachable here (it is `tab`) no longer costs
;; anything -- the digits are literal, not Shift-peeled.
(deflayer symbols-cmk
  _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _
  _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _
  _    S-1  S-2  S-3  S-4  S-5  -    7    8    9    =    _    _    _
  _    S-6  S-7  S-8  S-9  S-0  S-=  @np4 @np5 @np6 @npx _   _
  tab  [    ]    grv  _    _    .    1    2    3    /    _
  _    _    _    _    0    _
)

;; Right-Cmd-hold: arrows on the left home row, in hjkl order shifted one hand
;; over.
;;   a s d f  ->  left down up right
;; Shared by both alpha layouts -- the arrows are positional, and `j`/`k`/`l`/`;`
;; below are plain modifiers, so nothing here depends on which letters are live.
;;
;; Moving the layer key from the right index to the right thumb bought this
;; layer one key back. `j` used to sit under the same finger that was holding
;; the layer open and so was physically unpressable; it is now free, and carries
;; Right Cmd -- which is what the Voyager puts there (KC_RIGHT_GUI). Cmd-Left /
;; Cmd-Right for line ends therefore work from inside the layer instead of
;; needing physical Left Cmd, and all four right-hand modifiers are present.
;;
;; The left hand's own mods are shadowed (they ARE the arrows). The right hand's
;; are REPLACED, not left transparent: `j`/`k`/`l`/`;` are plain Cmd/Opt/Ctrl/
;; Shift rather than the @hmj..@hmsc tap-holds underneath, so Opt-Left (word),
;; Ctrl-Left and Shift-Left (select) fire instantly instead of waiting out
;; $hold-time. This is what the Voyager does -- its nav layer puts literal
;; KC_RIGHT_ALT / KC_RIGHT_CTRL / KC_RIGHT_SHIFT on the same three keys, not
;; mod-taps. Transparency looked equivalent and wasn't: it kept the 200ms wait
;; on a layer where every chord is a deliberate two-key reach anyway.
;; Cost: those four keys can no longer be TAPPED for their letters while
;; navigating. The Voyager gave that up too.
;;
;; The rest of the Voyager's layer 2 rides along, position for position:
;;   * Home / PgUp / PgDn / End down the inner column (physical 5 t g b).
;;   * Ctrl-Shift-Tab / Ctrl-Tab on physical n / m -- prev/next tab, right
;;     thumb holds the layer, right fingers switch.
;;   * Media transport on , . / and volume on o p [. These DUPLICATE the
;;     function row on purpose; they exist so the same finger does the same
;;     thing on both keyboards, nothing else.
;; The one group NOT ported is the mouse keys (movement on the Voyager's left
;; bottom row, buttons above the volume keys): kanata's movemouse needs
;; speed/acceleration tuning to not feel awful, and the trackpad is
;; centimeters away. u and i stay transparent because of it.
(deflayer nav
  _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _
  _    _    _    _    _    home _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _
  _    _    _    _    _    pgup _    _    _    vold volu mute _    _
  _    left down up   rght pgdn _    rmet ralt rctl rsft _    _
  _    _    _    _    _    end  C-S-tab C-tab prev next pp _
  _    _    _    _    _    _
)

;; Right-Option-hold: the real F1-F12, and the QWERTY/Colemak toggle on `\` --
;; far from everything else, because a stray press means typing garbage until
;; you notice.
;;
;; This layer used to be on Space-hold and used to carry a row of app slots
;; (@app1..@app7 emitting M-A-C-S-<n>). Space is the symbol layer now, and the
;; app slots are gone as duplicates: skhd binds the RAW Hyper chord, and Hyper
;; is already four home row mods held together (a+s+d+f, a+r+s+t in Colemak),
;; so Hyper + a number-row digit reaches open-app-slot.sh with no layer in the
;; path at all. That is also the only launcher path the Voyager can share,
;; since it sends Hyper from firmware.
;;
;; The name `launch` is now a small lie and is kept only because renaming it
;; touches nothing else useful.
(deflayer launch
  _    f1   f2   f3   f4   f5   f6   f7   f8   f9   f10  f11  f12
  _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _
  _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    @tog
  _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _
  _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _    _
  _    _    _    _    _    _
)
